From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: adbtrr_work6_pr_fixes_use <>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 12:51:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] os/mm: Bound CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_UAF_POISON_SIZE with a Kconfig
 range

The option had no range. mm_uaf_area() compares it against a size_t:

	nbytes = node->size - offset;
	if (nbytes > CONFIG_DEBUG_MM_UAF_POISON_SIZE) {

A negative value converts to a very large unsigned number, so the clamp
never fires and the whole chunk is poisoned and verified. That makes
every free and every malloc linear in the chunk size and, together with
the hex dump, turns a single detection into a very long report.

Add "range 4 4096" and document in the help text that the value is
rounded down to whole 32-bit words. The default of 64 is unchanged, so
existing configurations behave exactly as before.
---
 os/Kconfig.debug | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/os/Kconfig.debug b/os/Kconfig.debug
index e5e37933..6e934462 100644
--- a/os/Kconfig.debug
+++ b/os/Kconfig.debug
@@ -804,6 +804,7 @@ if DEBUG_MM_UAF
 config DEBUG_MM_UAF_POISON_SIZE
 	int "Poison pattern size (bytes)"
 	default 64
+	range 4 4096
 	---help---
 		Maximum number of bytes of the free chunk data area to fill with the
 		poison pattern. Only the data area which follows the free node
@@ -811,6 +812,12 @@ config DEBUG_MM_UAF_POISON_SIZE
 		covered. Larger values detect more corruption but slow down every
 		free and every malloc.
 
+		The value is rounded down to a whole number of 32-bit words. The
+		range is enforced because the value is compared against a size_t: a
+		negative value would convert to a huge unsigned number, defeat the
+		clamp and poison the whole chunk, which makes every free and every
+		malloc linear in the chunk size.
+
 config DEBUG_MM_UAF_DUMP_SIZE
 	int "Maximum bytes of a corrupted chunk to hex dump"
 	default 256
